\cf2 They took a break.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 With his morale lower than the Elvi who still forlornly believed he would rediscover his talents, Chanta sat down in the living room to a breakfast of three cheeseburgers and a supersize cola. He wasn\'92t all that hungry, so he said no to the fries.\
As Chanta mechanically chewed without tasting the soft bun, gooey cheese and juicy meat, he idly scrolled through a social network news feed without registering any of the content. His mind was not exactly an eight-lane freeway, more a winding mountain track gridlocked with thoughts of Sarah and Little Clinton. Why was Sarah\'92s baby so important to him? Was it simply because Little Clinton was Sarah\'92s or was there something else? Was the clone of a long-dead scientist truly whispering words of inspiration from the womb to Chanta? Was the warm sunny glow of Sarah\'92s belly\'97that no one but Chanta saw\'97real? Chanta thought of himself as neither particularly rational nor superstitious; he was too uneducated to be either. And he wasn\'92t particularly smart; he knew that. Street-smart perhaps. Street-wise. He always knew when he could steal and get away with it. His humour was coarse but it made people laugh. Street people. Not people like Sarah.\
Who was this Clinton Richard Dawkins anyway? Who was this guy, who those crazy Sages seemed to revere so much? So much they would plant an engineered clone foetus of him in Sarah. The 
\f2\i One After the One
\f1\i0 , they called him. 
\f2\i The true Dawkins will Create a universe of science and reason
\f1\i0 , Solo had said. Whoever Dawkins was, as a boy he\'92d loved Elvis, according to Kleo. Did Kleo mean the original Elvis, the one all the Elvi were cloned from?\
So many clones.\
Chanta chuckled through a morsel of cheeseburger and washed it down with some ice-diluted cola. He did love the Elvi, even before they started worshipping him, which Chanta found somewhat cringeworthy. None of it meant much without Sarah, though.\
The Elvi called Little Clinton the Dawkins, the One After the One. Very prophetic, messianic\'97though Chanta wasn\'92t entirely sure what those words meant in straight-up dictionary terms. He wondered if it had anything to do with the ELVIS warranty condition: never expose the hospitality unit to religion.\
\

\f2\i Save the Dawkins or we\'92ll blow you away.
\f1\i0 \
\
The Elvi threatened Sarah through song for considering abortion. Chanta was glad she reconsidered; somehow Little Clinton\'92s existence was guiding his fate. He was sure of it. If only Chanta could regain the psychic connection with Little Clinton when, fearing for his life, he desperately sang the first words that came to mind:\
\

\f2\i Evolve me tender,\
Evolve me strong
\f1\i0 \
\
Chanta\'92s four-chambered heart beat strong with the memory of it. He took a deep breath and, with a better-oxygenated brain, had a thought. He told his tablet, \'93Tell me everything you know about Clinton Richard Dawkins, a scientist from the 21st century.\'94 After a slight delay, the tablet display lit up with a heraldic-length scroll of entries. Chanta swiped through the ads for accountancy firms, plumbers, and real estate agents; he knew those would be fake links to scammers. Next were links about a rockabilly star called Stalkin\'92 Dawkins and a podcast called Talkin\'92 Dawkins. Chanta paused briefly over a link to something called the 
\f2\i Gawkin\'92 Dawkins Peep Show
\f1\i0 , shook his head, and kept scrolling\'85 and scrolling\'85 and scrolling, until he found such memes as 
\f2\i One does not simply read about Richard Dawkins
\f1\i0 , and quotes of Dawkins as an outspoken atheist, emeritus fellow, British biologist, and writer.\
He kept swiping\'85 Dawkins taught zoology. He sang to himself:\
\

\f2\i Happy Dawkins to you,\
You live in a zoo,\
You look like a donkey,\
And you\'92re British too.
\f1\i0 \
\
Chanta was delighted his lyrical abilities were returning, even if he was reasonably certain Clinton Dawkins looked and thought nothing like a donkey.\
\'93What are you singing about, Great Teacher?\'94 Vince the sound engineer asked as he plonked an enormous sundae on the table and sat across from Chanta.\
\'93Oh, ah, nothing. I was just researching this Dawkins character. Looking for some inspiration.\'94\
\'93The ancestor of The Holy Momma\'92s child?\'94\
\'93The one and only.\'94\
\'93Hey, that\'92s a good idea,\'94 said another Elvis, and took out his tablet.\
\'93There\'92s a lot of dross there, but now I\'92m looking at quotes from his books.\'94\
\'93Oh, yeah? Found anything interesting?\'94\
Chanta wasn\'92t sure yet. \'93Um, he seems to come down hard on religion\'85 
